### Step 1: Create a feature to count the number of unique ranks in the hand
python
# num_unique_ranks
# Feature description: Count the number of unique ranks among the five cards.
# Usefulness: This feature helps identify poker hands relying on rank diversity. For example, One Pair will have 4 unique ranks, Two Pairs will have 3, Three of a Kind will have 3, and so on. A lower number of unique ranks also indicates higher chances of hands like Full House or Four of a Kind.
df['num_unique_ranks'] = df[['V2', 'V4', 'V6', 'V8', 'V10']].nunique(axis=1)
end

### Step 2: Create a feature to determine if all cards are sequential (straight indicator)
python
# is_straight
# Feature description: A binary feature (1 or 0) indicating whether the hand forms a straight (5 sequential ranks).
# Usefulness: Straights are key poker hands. This feature directly identifies this pattern by checking if the rank differences between consecutive cards are exactly 1.
ranks = df[['V2', 'V4', 'V6', 'V8', 'V10']].apply(sorted, axis=1)  # Sort ranks within each hand
df['is_straight'] = ranks.apply(lambda row: all(row[i + 1] - row[i] == 1 for i in range(4))).astype(int)
end

### Step 3: Create a feature to count the maximum number of cards with the same suit
python
# max_suit_freq
# Feature description: Count the highest frequency of any suit in the hand.
# Usefulness: This feature identifies hands like Flush and Straight Flush, where all cards share the same suit. It also highlights suit patterns for other hands.
suits = df[['V1', 'V3', 'V5', 'V7', 'V9']]
df['max_suit_freq'] = suits.apply(lambda row: row.value_counts().max(), axis=1)
end

